<abstract>
<para>
&krdc; is a client application that allows you to view or even control
the desktop session on another machine that is running a compatible server.
<acronym>VNC</acronym>, <acronym>RDP</acronym>, and <acronym>SPICE</acronym>
are supported when the corresponding plugins are installed and enabled.
</para>
</abstract>

<keywordset>
<keyword>KDE</keyword>
<keyword>kdenetwork</keyword>
<keyword>krfb</keyword>
<keyword>VNC</keyword>
<keyword>RFB</keyword>
<keyword>krdc</keyword>
<keyword>Desktop Sharing</keyword>
<keyword>Remote Control</keyword>
<keyword>Remote Assistance</keyword>
<keyword>Remote Desktop</keyword>
<keyword>RDP</keyword>
<keyword>SPICE</keyword>
<keyword>Remote Administration</keyword>
</keywordset>

</bookinfo>

<chapter id="introduction">
<title>Introduction</title>

<para>&krdc; lets you view and control a desktop on another computer.
You can use it to work on a remote machine or help another user. The computer
you connect to must run a compatible remote desktop server; &krdc; is the
client and does not enable desktop sharing on that computer.</para>

<para>&krdc; supports <acronym>VNC</acronym> (Virtual Network Computing),
<acronym>RDP</acronym> (Remote Desktop Protocol), and <acronym>SPICE</acronym>.
The protocols available in your installation depend on the installed and enabled
plugins. For example, you can connect to a desktop shared by &krfb; using
<acronym>VNC</acronym>, to a Remote Desktop server using <acronym>RDP</acronym>,
or to a virtual machine with a <acronym>SPICE</acronym> server.</para>

<para>Start &krdc; from your application launcher or run
<userinput><command>krdc</command></userinput> in a terminal.
For command-line examples, see <xref linkend="command-line"/>.</para>
</chapter>

<chapter id="supported-protocols">
<title>Supported Protocols</title>

<para>&krdc; supports <acronym>VNC</acronym>, <acronym>RDP</acronym>, and
<acronym>SPICE</acronym> through protocol plugins. Select the protocol that
matches the server on the remote computer. The client and server can run
different operating systems.</para>

<sect1 id="protocol-vnc">
<title>VNC</title>

<para><acronym>VNC</acronym> uses the Remote Frame Buffer
(<acronym>RFB</acronym>) protocol. The server sends updates of its desktop image
to the client, and the client sends keyboard and pointer input back to the
server. It is commonly used to share an existing desktop session.</para>

<para><ulink url="https://apps.kde.org/krfb/">&krfb;</ulink> is &kde;'s
desktop sharing application and provides a <acronym>VNC</acronym> server for
sharing a &plasma; desktop. Start and configure &krfb; on the computer whose
desktop you want to share, then connect to it from &krdc; using
<acronym>VNC</acronym>. Other compatible VNC servers can also be used.</para>

<para>Connection speed and the amount of change on the remote screen affect
responsiveness. If updates are slow, try a lower <acronym>VNC</acronym> quality
setting in <xref linkend="vnc-settings"/>.</para>
</sect1>

<sect1 id="protocol-rdp">
<title>RDP</title>

<para><acronym>RDP</acronym> (Remote Desktop Protocol) provides remote access
to a desktop, with features such as audio playback and clipboard exchange.
Available features depend on the server and its configuration.</para>

<para>In &kde; &plasma;, <ulink url="https://invent.kde.org/plasma/krdp">
<application>KRDP</application></ulink> provides an RDP server for sharing a
Wayland desktop session. Configure remote desktop access in &plasma;'s
&systemsettings;, then connect from &krdc; using <acronym>RDP</acronym>.</para>

<para><acronym>RDP</acronym> is also used natively by &Microsoft; &Windows;
Remote Desktop. You can connect to a Windows PC after enabling Remote Desktop
on it. Hosting incoming connections requires a supported edition; Windows Home
does not include the Remote Desktop server.</para>

<para>For display, sound, sharing, gateway, and other connection options,
see <xref linkend="rdp-settings"/>.</para>
</sect1>

<sect1 id="protocol-spice">
<title>SPICE</title>

<para><acronym>SPICE</acronym> is commonly used to access the graphical console
of a virtual machine. <application>QEMU</application> can provide a SPICE server
for a guest, and tools such as <application>virt-manager</application> can
configure that server through <application>libvirt</application>.</para>

<para>Enable a SPICE display for the virtual machine and obtain its listening
address and port from the virtualization configuration. In &krdc;, connect to
the host running QEMU and the port assigned to that virtual machine. This
connects to the guest's console through the virtualization host, rather than
to a remote desktop server running inside the guest.</para>

<para>The guest agent, <application>spice-vdagent</application>, enables
integration features such as clipboard sharing and dynamic resolution changes.
See <xref linkend="spice-settings"/> for &krdc; options and the
<ulink url="https://www.spice-space.org/spice-user-manual.html">SPICE User
Manual</ulink> for QEMU, virt-manager, and guest configuration.</para>
</sect1>
</chapter>

<chapter id="using-krdc">
<title>Using &krdc;</title>

<para>
<screenshot>
<screeninfo>Here's a screenshot of &krdc;</screeninfo>
	<mediaobject>
	  <imageobject>
	    <imagedata fileref="krdc_mainwindow.png" format="PNG"/>
	  </imageobject>
	  <textobject>
	    <phrase>&krdc; main window</phrase>
	  </textobject>
	</mediaobject>
</screenshot>
</para>

<sect1 id="compatible-versions">
<title>Connecting &krdc; to compatible servers</title>

<para>Before connecting, enable the appropriate remote desktop server on the
remote computer and obtain its address and login details. The server must be
reachable from your computer, and its firewall must allow the required
connection.</para>

<procedure>
<step><para>Choose <menuchoice><guimenu>File</guimenu><guimenuitem>New
Connection</guimenuitem></menuchoice> to open the connection page.</para></step>
<step><para>Select <guilabel>vnc</guilabel>, <guilabel>rdp</guilabel>, or
<guilabel>spice</guilabel> in the protocol selector next to
<guilabel>Connect to:</guilabel>.</para></step>
<step><para>Enter the server address in the form
<userinput>[username@]host[:port]</userinput>, then press
<keycap>Enter</keycap> or click the arrow button next to the address.
The protocol is already selected, so do not prepend it to this field.</para></step>
<step><para>Review the host settings if prompted, and enter the credentials
required by the server. Each connection opens in a tab.</para></step>
</procedure>

<variablelist>
<varlistentry><term><acronym>VNC</acronym></term>
<listitem><para>Enter <userinput>megan</userinput> for the default port 5900.
For display 1 on port 5901, enter <userinput>megan:1</userinput> or
<userinput>megan:5901</userinput>. Port numbers below 100 are interpreted as
<acronym>VNC</acronym> display numbers and added to 5900.</para>

<para>
<screenshot>
<screeninfo>Entering a hostname into &krdc;</screeninfo>
	<mediaobject>
	  <imageobject>
	    <imagedata fileref="address_input.png" format="PNG"/>
	  </imageobject>
	  <textobject>
	    <phrase>Entering a hostname into &krdc;</phrase>
	  </textobject>
	</mediaobject>
</screenshot>
</para></listitem></varlistentry>
<varlistentry><term><acronym>RDP</acronym></term>
<listitem><para>Enter <userinput>megan</userinput> for the default port 3389,
or <userinput>megan:3390</userinput> for a different port. You can include a
user name, for example <userinput>alice@megan</userinput>.</para></listitem></varlistentry>
<varlistentry><term><acronym>SPICE</acronym></term>
<listitem><para>Enter the host and port of the virtual machine's
<acronym>SPICE</acronym> server, for example <userinput>vmhost:5902</userinput>.
The default port is 5900.</para></listitem></varlistentry>
</variablelist>

<para>The connection page also lists bookmarks, connection history, and
<acronym>VNC</acronym> services advertised on the local network through Zeroconf.
Typing in the address field filters this list. You can select a listed connection
and use <guimenuitem>Connect</guimenuitem> from its context menu.
The <guilabel>Remote Desktops</guilabel> panel provides another way to find saved
and discovered connections.</para>
</sect1>

<sect1 id="connection">
<title>What happens when you connect</title>

<para>The <guilabel>Host Configuration</guilabel> dialog contains settings for
the selected protocol. Clear <guilabel>Show this dialog again for this
host</guilabel> to reuse them without being prompted on each connection.
Settings are associated with the connection address; connecting by a different
host name or IP address can use a different configuration.</para>

<para>
<screenshot>
<screeninfo>&krdc; connection speed selection</screeninfo>
	<mediaobject>
	  <imageobject>
	    <imagedata fileref="vnc_host_configuration.png" format="PNG"/>
	  </imageobject>
	  <textobject>
	    <phrase>&krdc; connection speed selection</phrase>
	  </textobject>
	</mediaobject>
</screenshot>
</para>

<para>When the server requests authentication, enter your password and, if
requested, your user name. The <guilabel>Remember password</guilabel> option
allows &krdc; to store credentials through the system credential store using
QtKeychain. Password storage also depends on the global
<guilabel>Remember passwords</guilabel> setting.</para>

<para>
<screenshot>
<screeninfo>&krdc; password entry</screeninfo>
	<mediaobject>
	  <imageobject>
	    <imagedata fileref="password_entry.png" format="PNG"/>
	  </imageobject>
	  <textobject>
	    <phrase>&krdc; password entry</phrase>
	  </textobject>
	</mediaobject>
</screenshot>
</para>

<para>For <acronym>RDP</acronym>, &krdc; may ask you to verify an unknown or
changed server certificate. Check the certificate details with the server
administrator before accepting it. The dialog offers
<guilabel>Remember this certificate</guilabel> for subsequent connections.</para>

<para>The tab icon indicates connection status. After authentication succeeds,
you can view or control the remote desktop.</para>
</sect1>

<sect1 id="controlling-remote">
<title>Controlling the remote desktop connection</title>

<para>Use the keyboard and mouse in a connected tab to work on the remote
desktop. The toolbar shown below provides controls for opening connections,
changing the view, and interacting with the remote desktop. Buttons may be
unavailable depending on the protocol and connection state.</para>

<para>
<screenshot>
<screeninfo>&krdc; toolbar</screeninfo>
	<mediaobject>
	  <imageobject>
	    <imagedata fileref="view-toolbar.png" format="PNG"/>
	  </imageobject>
	  <textobject>
	    <phrase>&krdc; toolbar</phrase>
	  </textobject>
	</mediaobject>
</screenshot>
</para>

<para>From left to right, the toolbar contains the following buttons:</para>

<variablelist>
<varlistentry>
<term><guibutton>New Connection</guibutton></term>
<listitem><para>Open the connection page to choose a protocol and enter a server address.
The new connection opens in a tab, so you can keep other sessions open.</para></listitem>
</varlistentry>
<varlistentry>
<term><guibutton>Full Screen</guibutton></term>
<listitem><para>Display the remote desktop in full screen. The button changes to
<guibutton>Window Mode</guibutton>, which returns to the normal window.
In full screen, use the floating toolbar to access session controls.
<guimenuitem>Stick Toolbar</guimenuitem> keeps it visible, and
<guimenuitem>Minimize Full Screen Window</guimenuitem> gives access to your local desktop.</para></listitem>
</varlistentry>
<varlistentry>
<term><guibutton>Screenshot</guibutton></term>
<listitem><para>Copy an image of the remote desktop to the local clipboard.
Paste it into an image editor or another application to save it.</para></listitem>
</varlistentry>
<varlistentry>
<term><guibutton>View Only</guibutton></term>
<listitem><para>Observe the remote desktop without sending keyboard or mouse input.
Clipboard sharing is also suppressed while this option is enabled.
Click the button again to resume controlling the remote desktop.</para></listitem>
</varlistentry>
<varlistentry>
<term><guibutton>Clipboard Sharing</guibutton></term>
<listitem><para>Enable or disable clipboard exchange between your computer and the
remote desktop. Supported content depends on the protocol and server.
Clipboard exchange requires <guibutton>View Only</guibutton> to be turned off.</para></listitem>
</varlistentry>
<varlistentry>
<term><guibutton>Local Cursor</guibutton></term>
<listitem><para>Show a local mouse pointer to help track its position over the remote
desktop. Click the button again to turn this option off.</para></listitem>
</varlistentry>
<varlistentry>
<term><guibutton>Grab Keys</guibutton></term>
<listitem><para>Send as many keyboard shortcuts as possible to the remote desktop.
Some shortcuts may remain reserved by the local desktop or window system.
Turn this option off to handle shortcuts locally again. Automatic keyboard
grabbing on entering full screen can be enabled in the general settings.</para></listitem>
</varlistentry>
<varlistentry>
<term><guibutton>Scale</guibutton></term>
<listitem><para>Scale the remote image to fit the available window area.
Turn this option off to view the desktop without scaling. &krdc; remembers
separate scaling choices for window and full screen modes. This changes the
displayed image size, rather than the remote desktop resolution.</para></listitem>
</varlistentry>
<varlistentry>
<term><guibutton>Disconnect</guibutton></term>
<listitem><para>Close the current connection. Closing its tab also disconnects it.
This does not issue a logout or shutdown command to the remote computer;
what happens to the remote session depends on the server.</para></listitem>
</varlistentry>
</variablelist>
</sect1>

<sect1 id="bookmarks">
<title>Bookmarks and connection history</title>

<para>To save a connection, choose
<menuchoice><guimenu>Bookmarks</guimenu><guimenuitem>Add Bookmark</guimenuitem></menuchoice>.
Open it again from the <guimenu>Bookmarks</guimenu> menu or the connection list.</para>

<para>
<screenshot>
<screeninfo>&krdc; bookmarks menu</screeninfo>
	<mediaobject>
	  <imageobject>
	    <imagedata fileref="bookmarks_menu.png" format="PNG"/>
	  </imageobject>
	  <textobject>
	    <phrase>&krdc; bookmarks menu</phrase>
	  </textobject>
	</mediaobject>
</screenshot>
</para>

<para>When <guilabel>Remember connection history</guilabel> is enabled,
successful connections are recorded under
<menuchoice><guimenu>Bookmarks</guimenu><guimenuitem>History</guimenuitem></menuchoice>
and appear in the connection list. Right-click a saved entry in the list to
connect, rename it, change its settings, or delete it.</para>

<para>For a bookmarked connection, the settings dialog can also edit its
<guilabel>Name:</guilabel> and <guilabel>URL:</guilabel>. Use a complete URL such
as <userinput>rdp://alice@megan:3389</userinput>, keeping the same protocol.
Changes to connection settings may take effect only when you reconnect.</para>
</sect1>

<sect1 id="managing-configuration">
<title>Managing &krdc; configuration</title>

<para>Open <menuchoice><guimenu>Settings</guimenu><guimenuitem>Configure
KRDC...</guimenuitem></menuchoice> to change application preferences.</para>

<para>
<screenshot>
<screeninfo>&krdc; preferences - General tab</screeninfo>
	<mediaobject>
	  <imageobject>
	    <imagedata fileref="general_preferences.png" format="PNG"/>
	  </imageobject>
	  <textobject>
	    <phrase>&krdc; preferences - <guilabel>General</guilabel> tab</phrase>
	  </textobject>
	</mediaobject>
</screenshot>
</para>

<variablelist>
<varlistentry><term><guilabel>General</guilabel></term>
<listitem><para>Choose whether to restore open sessions on startup, remember
history and passwords, display a system tray icon or status bar, and preserve
the aspect ratio when scaling. You can also configure the default protocol,
initial host configuration dialog, window resizing, automatic full screen and
keyboard grabbing, and tab appearance and closing behavior.</para></listitem></varlistentry>
<varlistentry><term><guilabel>Hosts</guilabel></term>
<listitem><para>Select a saved host and click <guibutton>Configure...</guibutton>
to change its protocol settings. <guibutton>Remove</guibutton> deletes the
saved host configuration. Bookmarks and connection history are managed
separately.</para></listitem></varlistentry>
<varlistentry><term><guilabel>Plugins</guilabel></term>
<listitem><para>Enable or disable installed protocol plugins. Restart &krdc;
after changing plugin availability. The configuration button of a protocol
plugin opens its default settings.</para></listitem></varlistentry>
</variablelist>
</sect1>

<sect1 id="vnc-settings">
<title>VNC settings</title>

<para>
<screenshot>
<screeninfo>&krdc; preferences - <guilabel>VNC</guilabel> tab</screeninfo>
	<mediaobject>
	  <imageobject>
	    <imagedata fileref="vnc_preferences.png" format="PNG"/>
	  </imageobject>
	  <textobject>
	    <phrase>&krdc; preferences - <guilabel>VNC</guilabel> profiles tab</phrase>
	  </textobject>
	</mediaobject>
</screenshot>
</para>

<para><guilabel>Connection type:</guilabel> offers
<guilabel>High Quality (LAN, direct connection)</guilabel>,
<guilabel>Medium Quality (DSL, Cable, fast Internet)</guilabel>, and
<guilabel>Low Quality (Modem, ISDN, slow Internet)</guilabel>.
Choose a quality appropriate to the whole connection, including the remote
computer's network link. A lower quality can help on slow connections.</para>

<para><guilabel>Scale to Size:</guilabel> sets the size used to display the
remote desktop, with predefined and custom dimensions. The password-manager
option prevents sending clipboard content marked as a password by supported
password managers, such as KeePassXC. It cannot identify passwords copied by
applications that do not provide that marker.</para>
</sect1>

<sect1 id="rdp-settings">
<title>RDP settings</title>

<para>The RDP configuration dialog contains the following tabs. Changes to
connection settings may take effect only after reconnecting.</para>

<para><emphasis role="bold">General</emphasis></para>

<para>
<screenshot>
<screeninfo>&krdc; RDP preferences - General tab</screeninfo>
	<mediaobject>
	  <imageobject>
	    <imagedata fileref="rdp_preferences_general.png" format="PNG"/>
	  </imageobject>
	  <textobject>
	    <phrase>&krdc; preferences - <guilabel>RDP</guilabel> general tab</phrase>
	  </textobject>
	</mediaobject>
</screenshot>
</para>

<para>The <guilabel>General</guilabel> tab controls the appearance of the
remote desktop, input devices, and resources shared with the remote session.</para>

<para><guilabel>Resolution</guilabel> selects the desktop size requested from
the server. Choose a preset, the current window or screen size, or a custom
width and height. <guilabel>Use All Screens Resolution</guilabel> requests a
multi-monitor session; in full screen, the remote monitors are displayed on
the corresponding local screens. Availability depends on the server and your
local display configuration.</para>

<para><guilabel>Scale window contents to match resolution</guilabel> controls
local image scaling. <guilabel>Desktop scale factor:</guilabel> and
<guilabel>Device scale factor:</guilabel> instead specify scaling information
sent to the server. Use these to adjust the size of remote interface elements
on high-resolution displays, where supported by the server.</para>

<para><guilabel>Acceleration:</guilabel> selects the graphics encoding mode,
including H.264, RemoteFX, or disabling acceleration.
<guilabel>Color depth:</guilabel> controls the number of colors used for the
remote desktop; some acceleration modes require 32-bit color.
<guilabel>Keyboard layout:</guilabel> should match the keyboard you use so that
key presses are interpreted correctly.</para>

<para><guilabel>Sound:</guilabel> selects playback on this computer, playback
on the remote computer, or no sound. <guilabel>Share Media:</guilabel> makes a
local directory available to the remote session; leave it empty to disable
drive sharing. <guilabel>Share Smartcard:</guilabel> specifies the smartcard
name to redirect to the server.</para>

<para><guilabel>Admin (or console) session</guilabel> requests an administrative
session on servers that support it. <guilabel>Prevent remote session
lock</guilabel> sends periodic simulated mouse activity to keep the session
active; server policies may still enforce locking.</para>

<para><emphasis role="bold">Security</emphasis></para>

<para>
<screenshot>
<screeninfo>&krdc; RDP preferences - Security tab</screeninfo>
	<mediaobject>
	  <imageobject>
	    <imagedata fileref="rdp_preferences_security.png" format="PNG"/>
	  </imageobject>
	  <textobject>
	    <phrase>&krdc; preferences - <guilabel>RDP</guilabel> security tab</phrase>
	  </textobject>
	</mediaobject>
</screenshot>
</para>

<para>The <guilabel>Security</guilabel> tab controls which security methods
&krdc; offers when establishing the RDP connection. The
<guilabel>NLA</guilabel>, <guilabel>TLS</guilabel>, <guilabel>RDP</guilabel>,
and <guilabel>Ext</guilabel> check boxes enable the corresponding FreeRDP
security modes. More than one can be enabled so that the client and server
can negotiate a compatible method.</para>

<para><guilabel>TLS security level:</guilabel> sets the cryptographic security
level required for TLS connections. A higher level imposes stricter
requirements and may prevent connections to older servers.</para>

<para><guilabel>Authentication Package List:</guilabel> passes a custom
selection of authentication packages to FreeRDP. Leave it empty to use the
default behavior, or enter the value supplied by your administrator. Change
security options to match the server's requirements.</para>

<para><emphasis role="bold">Proxy</emphasis></para>

<para>
<screenshot>
<screeninfo>&krdc; RDP preferences - Proxy tab</screeninfo>
	<mediaobject>
	  <imageobject>
	    <imagedata fileref="rdp_preferences_proxy.png" format="PNG"/>
	  </imageobject>
	  <textobject>
	    <phrase>&krdc; preferences - <guilabel>RDP</guilabel> proxy tab</phrase>
	  </textobject>
	</mediaobject>
</screenshot>
</para>

<para>The <guilabel>Proxy</guilabel> tab configures a network proxy used for
the connection. Select <guilabel>HTTP</guilabel> or <guilabel>SOCKS</guilabel>
in <guilabel>Protocol:</guilabel>, then enter the proxy address in
<guilabel>Server:</guilabel> using the form <userinput>host:port</userinput>.</para>

<para>If the proxy requires authentication, fill in
<guilabel>Username:</guilabel> and <guilabel>Password:</guilabel> with the proxy
credentials. These may differ from the credentials for the remote desktop.
Leave <guilabel>Server:</guilabel> empty when no proxy is needed. The target
remote desktop address remains the address entered on the connection page.</para>

<para><emphasis role="bold">Gateway</emphasis></para>

<para>
<screenshot>
<screeninfo>&krdc; RDP preferences - Gateway tab</screeninfo>
	<mediaobject>
	  <imageobject>
	    <imagedata fileref="rdp_preferences_gateway.png" format="PNG"/>
	  </imageobject>
	  <textobject>
	    <phrase>&krdc; preferences - <guilabel>RDP</guilabel> gateway tab</phrase>
	  </textobject>
	</mediaobject>
</screenshot>
</para>

<para>The <guilabel>Gateway</guilabel> tab configures access through an RDP
gateway, which provides a route to a remote desktop on another network.
Enter the gateway address in <guilabel>Server:</guilabel> as
<userinput>host:port</userinput>, using the port supplied by your administrator.
Keep the destination computer's address on the main connection page.</para>

<para>Enter the gateway account's <guilabel>Domain:</guilabel>,
<guilabel>Username:</guilabel>, and <guilabel>Password:</guilabel> as required.
These fields authenticate access to the gateway; the remote desktop may
require separate credentials.</para>

<para><guilabel>Transport type:</guilabel> offers <guilabel>Auto</guilabel>,
<guilabel>HTTP</guilabel>, and <guilabel>RPC</guilabel>. Use
<guilabel>Auto</guilabel> unless your administrator specifies a transport.
Leave the gateway <guilabel>Server:</guilabel> field empty when connecting
without an RDP gateway.</para>

<para>If SSH tunnel support is available, an additional
<guilabel>SSH tunnel</guilabel> tab appears in the host configuration dialog.
See <xref linkend="ssh-tunnels"/> for its options.</para>
</sect1>

<sect1 id="spice-settings">
<title>SPICE settings</title>

<para>
<screenshot>
<screeninfo>&krdc; preferences - <guilabel>SPICE</guilabel> tab</screeninfo>
	<mediaobject>
	  <imageobject>
	    <imagedata fileref="spice_preferences.png" format="PNG"/>
	  </imageobject>
	  <textobject>
	    <phrase>&krdc; preferences - <guilabel>SPICE</guilabel> profiles tab</phrase>
	  </textobject>
	</mediaobject>
</screenshot>
</para>

<para>Choose the requested remote resolution from the presets, the current
window or screen size, or custom dimensions. Dynamic resizing requires
<application>spice-vdagent</application> in the guest. You can also enable local
scaling and audio playback. Clipboard exchange depends on support in the guest.</para>
</sect1>

<sect1 id="ssh-tunnels">
<title>SSH tunnels</title>

<para>
<screenshot>
<screeninfo>&krdc; preferences - <guilabel>SSH</guilabel> tab</screeninfo>
	<mediaobject>
	  <imageobject>
	    <imagedata fileref="sshtunnel_preferences.png" format="PNG"/>
	  </imageobject>
	  <textobject>
	    <phrase>&krdc; preferences - <guilabel>SSH</guilabel> tab</phrase>
	  </textobject>
	</mediaobject>
</screenshot>
</para>

<para>If your installation includes SSH tunnel support, the host settings offer
<guilabel>Connect via SSH tunnel</guilabel>. Enter the SSH port and user name
for the same host as the remote desktop server. SSH authentication is separate
from remote desktop authentication.</para>

<para><guilabel>Tunnel via loopback address</guilabel> makes the tunnel connect
to the remote desktop service through the remote host's loopback address.
Use this when the service listens only on that address. The remote desktop port
still comes from the connection address.</para>
</sect1>


<sect1 id="command-line">
<title>Starting connections from the command line</title>
<para>Pass a complete URL to connect directly:</para>
<screen><userinput><command>krdc</command> rdp://alice@megan</userinput>
<userinput><command>krdc</command> vnc://megan:5901</userinput>
<userinput><command>krdc</command> spice://vmhost:5902</userinput></screen>

<para>Use <option>--fullscreen</option> with one URL to start that connection
in full screen. You can also pass several URLs to open multiple connections:</para>
<screen><userinput><command>krdc</command> --fullscreen rdp://megan</userinput>
<userinput><command>krdc</command> vnc://megan rdp://workstation</userinput></screen>

<para>In a complete URL, percent-encode reserved characters in the user name.
For example, use <userinput>rdp://alice%40example.org@megan</userinput> for the
user name <literal>alice@example.org</literal>.</para>

<para>&krdc; can read basic connection details from an <filename>.rdp</filename>
file passed on the command line:</para>
<screen><userinput><command>krdc</command> connection.rdp</userinput></screen>
<para>This reads the server address, user name, domain, and a password field
if present; it does not import every setting supported by other RDP clients.
Use the host configuration dialog for other options. Run
<userinput><command>krdc</command> <option>--help</option></userinput> for
command-line help.</para>
</sect1>
</chapter>

<chapter id="faq">
<title>Questions and Answers</title>
<qandaset id="faqlist">
<qandaentry>
<question><para>Why is a protocol missing or an address rejected?</para></question>
<answer><para>Check that the corresponding plugin is installed and enabled in
<guilabel>Plugins</guilabel>, then restart &krdc;. Check the selected protocol
and address syntax in <xref linkend="compatible-versions"/>.
RDP support uses the FreeRDP libraries directly; it does not require launching
an external <command>xfreerdp</command> executable.</para></answer>
</qandaentry>
<qandaentry>
<question><para>Why can I not connect to the remote computer?</para></question>
<answer><para>Check the host name, port, network or VPN connection, and whether
the remote desktop server is running. Ensure its firewall permits the
connection. For authentication failures, check your user name, password, domain,
and permission to log in remotely. If you use a proxy, gateway, or SSH tunnel,
check its settings and credentials too.</para></answer>
</qandaentry>
<qandaentry>
<question><para>Why can I see the desktop but not control it?</para></question>
<answer><para>Turn off <guimenuitem>View Only</guimenuitem> in the
<guimenu>Session</guimenu> menu. The server may also restrict your connection
to viewing; ask the person administering the remote desktop to check its
permissions.</para></answer>
</qandaentry>
<qandaentry>
<question><para>Why does copying and pasting not work?</para></question>
<answer><para>Enable <guimenuitem>Clipboard Sharing</guimenuitem>, disable
<guimenuitem>View Only</guimenuitem>, and check that the server supports the
content you are copying. For VNC, the password-manager filter can deliberately
block copied passwords. For SPICE, check the guest agent.</para></answer>
</qandaentry>
<qandaentry>
<question><para>How can I change settings after hiding the host dialog?</para></question>
<answer><para>Open <guilabel>Hosts</guilabel> in the application preferences,
select the host, and click <guibutton>Configure...</guibutton>. You can enable
<guilabel>Show this dialog again for this host</guilabel> there.</para></answer>
</qandaentry>
<qandaentry>
<question><para>Where can I report a problem?</para></question>
<answer><para>Report bugs at <ulink url="https://bugs.kde.org">the &kde; bug
tracker</ulink>. Include your &krdc; version, protocol, local operating system,
server software, and steps to reproduce the problem. Omit passwords and other
private connection details.</para></answer>
</qandaentry>
</qandaset>
</chapter>

